# Line endings are normalised to LF in the repository, and checked out as LF
# everywhere including Windows.
#
# Without this, Git for Windows' default (core.autocrlf=true) rewrites every
# file to CRLF on checkout, and `prettier --check` — which expects LF — fails on
# 108 files in a fresh clone before a contributor has touched anything. The same
# default is what let 40 files come to be *stored* with CRLF, which made an
# editor's save look like a 1,200-line diff of untouched code.
#
# eol=lf rather than plain text=auto: the latter still hands Windows CRLF on
# checkout, which is the half that breaks the tooling.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so Git must never look for line endings in them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.npy binary
*.index binary
*.faiss binary
